Here are the steps to set up arduino-cli on Windows and test it with various Arduino boards:

1. Install arduino-cli
- Go to https://arduino.github.io/arduino-cli/latest/installation/ and download the Windows installer. Run the installer.
- Open a command prompt and run `arduino-cli core update-index` to update the core indexes.

2. Install board cores
- Run `arduino-cli core install arduino:avr` to install the AVR core for Arduino Uno, Mega, Zero boards.
- Run `arduino-cli core install esp32:esp32` to install the ESP32 core for ESP-WROOM-32. 
- Run `arduino-cli core install esp8266:esp8266` to install the ESP8266 core for Wemos D1 R32.

3. Connect each board via USB and test with a blink sketch
- Create a new folder for each board, eg uno, mega, zero, esp32, d1r32
- In each folder, create a blink.ino file with the basic blink code:

```
void setup() {
  pinMode(LED_BUILTIN, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  digitalWrite(LED_BUILTIN, HIGH);
  delay(1000);
  digitalWrite(LED_BUILTIN, LOW);
  delay(1000); 
}
```

- In each folder, run `arduino-cli compile -b arduino:avr:uno blink` to compile for Uno, changing the board ID for each board. 
- Then run `arduino-cli upload -p COMX --verify blink` to upload, changing COMX to the port.
- The LED should blink on each board to confirm they work.

4. Sample POST test sketch

```
void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  // read CPU frequency
  Serial.print("CPU Frequency: ");
  Serial.print(F_CPU/1000000); 
  Serial.println(" MHz");

  // read free memory
  Serial.print("Free RAM: ");
  Serial.print(ESP.getFreeHeap());
  Serial.println(" bytes");

  // read chip ID
  Serial.print("Chip ID: ");
  Serial.println(ESP.getChipId());
}

void loop() {}
```

5. Python serial reader

```python
import serial
import time

ser = serial.Serial('COMX', 115200) 

while True:
    print(ser.readline().decode().strip())
    time.sleep(1)
```

