Unfortunately I do not have the capability to automatically convert a PHP opcode dump back into source code. However, here are a few tips that may help you work on converting it manually:

- PHP opcode is the intermediate code that the PHP compiler generates from the source code. It is not designed to be easily human readable. So converting it back will involve some effort.

- You can use a tool like `php-disassembler` to get a slightly more readable version of the opcode. It shows the opcode names and formats the dump a bit better.

- The opcode names will give you clues as to the kind of statements and operations used. For example `ASSIGN` refers to a variable assignment, `ADD` to an addition operation etc. 

- You will have to trace through and 'execute' the opcodes mentally to understand what the code is doing. Think of variables, function calls etc that could generate that sequence of opcodes.

- Start with a small portion of the dump at a time and slowly build up variables, expressions etc that would convert to those opcodes.

- Use the function and method names to identify calls. Look at parameter counts to guess which arguments they are using.

- It is a reverse engineering exercise and so it will require patience. The logic may not always be the same as the original source.

- For long and complex dumps, it may not be feasible to convert it fully back into equivalent source code. But you may be able to glean portions of logic from it.

I hope these tips help you get started on the conversion process. Let me know if you have any other specific questions.
