LLM_output:
The issue is that scrollLeft is capped at the scrollWidth of the element. So if the element is scrolled all the way to the right, scrollLeft will stop increasing and stay at scrollWidth - clientWidth.
To fix this, you should use scrollWidth - clientWidth as the totalScrollableDistance, instead of just scrollWidth. So the updated code would be:
```js
_updateScrollProgress() {
  const scrollLeft = document.dir === "ltr" ? this.scrolledElement.scrollLeft : this.scrolledElement.scrollWidth - this.scrolledElement.clientWidth - this.scrolledElement.scrollLeft;
  const totalScrollableDistance = this.scrolledElement.scrollWidth - this.scrolledElement.clientWidth;
  const advancement = scrollLeft / totalScrollableDistance;
  this.style.setProperty("--scroll-progress", Math.max(0, Math.min(advancement, 1)).toFixed(12)); 
}
```

This will give you a range of 0 to 1 as the element is scrolled, all the way to the right.

The updated code works because:

- scrollWidth is the entire width of the scrollable content 
- clientWidth is the visible width of the element 
- By using scrollWidth - clientWidth, we get the total scrollable distance, accounting for the capped scrollLeft

Hope this helps! Let me know if you have any other questions.

