1) The region D is bounded by the curves y = 2/3x and y = 2√x. To set up the integral, x will be the variable of integration and y will be a function of x.
2) The limits of integration for x are from 0 to 1, since that is where the two bounding curves intersect. 
3) To evaluate the integral, we calculate:
\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}{\left[\int_{2\sqrt{x}}^{2/3x}{(2y{x^2} + 9{y^3})dy}\right]dx}
4) Integrating the inner integral term-by-term, we get: 
\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}{\left[x^2y^2 + 3y^4\right]_{2\sqrt{x}}^{2/3x}dx}
5) Substituting the limits of integration and simplifying, the final integral is:
\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}{\left(\frac{8}{9}x+\frac{128}{27}x^2-\frac{16}{3}\sqrt{x}-\frac{64}{9}x^{3/2}\right)dx}
6) Evaluating this integral, we get the final answer: \boxed{\frac{32}{27}}

