Here is an implementation of a function to generate all permutations of a list in Python without using built-in library functions:

```python
def permutations(elements):
    if len(elements) <=1:
        yield elements
    else:
        for perm in permutations(elements[1:]):
            for i in range(len(elements)):
                yield perm[:i] + elements[0:1] + perm[i:]
```

To explain, it uses recursion to systematically generate all permutations. The base case is when the list only has 1 or 0 elements, in which case there is just one permutation - the original elements. 

Otherwise, it recursively generates all permutations of the elements excluding the first element. Then it iterates through each existing permutation, and inserts the first element at every possible position, yielding a new permutation each time.

So this generates all unique rearrangements of the list elements, which is exactly all the permutations. It does not use any imported functions like itertools.permutations, working entirely through native Python.

To use it:

```python
elements = [1, 2, 3] 
for perm in permutations(elements):
    print(perm)
```

So it generates all permutations of the list [1, 2, 3].

