Creating an interactive website with rotating animation involves HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Here's an example of how you can achieve this:

HTML:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
    <div id="triangle"></div>
    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

CSS (styles.css):

```css
#triangle {
    width: 0;
    height: 0;
    border-left: 50px solid transparent;
    border-right: 50px solid transparent;
    border-bottom: 100px solid red;
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    transform-origin: 50% 50%;
}
```

This CSS will create a triangle by using borders. The triangle will be centered on the page.

JavaScript (script.js):

```javascript
let deg = 0;
setInterval(() => {
    document.getElementById('triangle').style.transform = `rotate(${deg}deg)`;
    deg = (deg + 360/60) % 360;
}, 1000);
```

This JavaScript code will rotate the triangle 6 degrees every second, which will result in a complete rotation every minute (60 seconds). The rotation is around the center of the triangle.

Please note that the triangle will rotate counter-clockwise because of the way the rotation is defined in the CSS transform property. To make it rotate clockwise, you can simply change the sign of the degrees in the JavaScript code:

```javascript
deg = (deg - 360/60) % 360;
```

