You can create valid jumpmarks/anchors for your URLs by converting your section titles into a URL-friendly format, often referred to as a "slug". This involves removing special characters, replacing spaces with hyphens or underscores, and lowercasing the string. Here's an example of how you could do it in PHP:

```php
function create_slug($string){
   $string = strtolower($string); // convert to lowercase
   $string = preg_replace("/[^a-z0-9_\s-]/", "", $string); // remove unwanted characters
   $string = preg_replace("/[\s-]+/", " ", $string); // consolidate multiple spaces and hyphens
   $string = preg_replace("/[\s_]/", "-", $string); // convert spaces and underscores to hyphens
   return $string;
}
$title = "Social Engineering - How can I detect Social Engineering Attempts?";
$url = "http://example.com/#" . create_slug($title);
```

In this code, `create_slug` is a function that takes a string and converts it into a URL-friendly format. The resulting string can then be appended to a URL with a '#' to create a jumpmark.

Note: This code assumes that the input string is in English or a similar language that uses the Latin alphabet. If you're working with other languages or scripts, you might need to modify the function to handle different sets of characters.

To find the shortest path in a graph, we can use Dijkstra's algorithm. Here's a simple Java program that demonstrates Dijkstra's algorithm to find the shortest path between two nodes in a weighted graph:

```java
import java.util.*;

class Edge {
    int from, to, weight;

    public Edge(int from, int to, int weight) {
        this.from = from;
        this.to = to;
        this.weight = weight;
    }
}

public class ShortestPath {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 6;
        List<Edge> edges = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(
                new Edge(0, 1, 5),
                new Edge(0, 2, 1),
                new Edge(1, 3, 3),
                new Edge(2, 3, 6),
                new Edge(2, 4, 6),
                new Edge(3, 5, 1),
                new Edge(4, 5, 2)
        ));

        int startNode = 0;
        int endNode = 5;

        int[] shortestPath = dijkstra(n, edges, startNode);
        System.out.println("Shortest path from node " + startNode + " to node " + endNode + " is: " + shortestPath[endNode]);
    }

    static int[] dijkstra(int n, List<Edge> edges, int startNode) {
        Map<Integer, List<Edge>> graph = new HashMap<>();
        for (Edge edge : edges) {
            if (!graph.containsKey(edge.from)) {
                graph.put(edge.from, new ArrayList<>());
            }
            graph.get(edge.from).add(edge);
        }

        PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(a -> a[1]));
        pq.offer(new int[]{startNode, 0});

        int[] distances = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(distances, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        distances[startNode] = 0;

        Set<Integer> visited = new HashSet<>();

        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
            int[] curr = pq.poll();
            int node = curr[0];
            int distance = curr[1];

            if (visited.contains(node)) continue;
            visited.add(node);

            if (graph.containsKey(node)) {
                for (Edge edge : graph.get(node)) {
                    int newDistance = distance + edge.weight;
                    if (newDistance < distances[edge.to]) {
                        distances[edge.to] = newDistance;
                        pq.offer(new int[]{edge.to, newDistance});
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return distances;
    }
}
```

This program defines a simple Edge class to represent the graph's edges and uses Dijkstra's algorithm to find the shortest path between two nodes. The example graph contains 6 nodes, and the program calculates the shortest path from node 0 to node 5. The output will display the shortest path distance.

You can modify the graph edges and nodes, as well as the start and end nodes, to test different scenarios.

